  "result_text": "# Track 28 — Accessibility & Internationalization of Reports\n\n**Scope:** desk audit only, against the design as documented (CONTEXT.md / CLAUDE.md / schema-lock-m1.md). No `report/generator.py` source was available in this session — findings are against the documented design surface: self-contained Jinja2 HTML, system-font-stack CSS, print media query, base64-inlined charts/images, zero external dependencies, Model Cards/Datasheets structure, Core Four metric table (metric_name, subgroup, subgroup_sample_size, metric_value, ci_lower, ci_upper, p_value, insufficient_sample), EU AI Act / NIST RMF tagging per row.\n\n---\n\n## Part 1 — WCAG 2.1 AA Desk Audit\n\n### 1.1 Color contrast (SC 1.4.3, SC 1.4.11)\n**Gap:** the design implies pass/fail or flagged-vs-not styling on metric rows (disparity flagged, `insufficient_sample=True`, backend-divergence flag). If this is color-only (red/green fill), it fails SC 1.4.1 (Use of Color) and risks SC 1.4.11 (Non-text Contrast, 3:1 minimum) if the flag is a colored badge/icon rather than text.\n**Fix:**\n- Every flag state ships as text + icon + color, never color alone: `⚠ Insufficient sample (n=12)`, `✕ Flagged — DIR 0.62 < 0.80`, `✓ No disparity detected`.\n- Badge background/border must hit 3:1 contrast against the page background; badge text must hit 4.5:1 (normal) against the badge fill.\n- Fix the palette at design time, don't rely on default red/green: e.g. flagged = `#B3261E` on `#FCEEEE` (contrast ≈ 7.7:1), pass = `#1E6B3C` on `#EAF5EE` (≈ 7.9:1), insufficient = `#7A5B00` on `#FDF3D9` (≈ 6.9:1) — avoid pure `#FF0000`/`#00FF00` which under-perform against white/light-gray table backgrounds and fail for red-green color-blind readers regardless of ratio.\n\n### 1.2 Keyboard navigability (SC 2.1.1, SC 2.4.7)\n**Gap:** if Track 26 adds interactivity (collapsible sections, tabbed metric views, SHAP chart drill-down), a static-HTML-first codebase commonly ships click-only `onclick` handlers with no `tabindex`, no `role`, and no visible focus ring (many resets do `*:focus{outline:none}` for aesthetics).\n**Fix:**\n- Any collapsible/expand control must be a real `<button>` (or `<summary>/<details>`, which is keyboard-accessible by default and needs zero JS) — not a `<div onclick>`.\n- Preserve a visible focus indicator (`:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid <brand-color>; outline-offset: 2px; }`); never `outline: none` without a replacement.\n- Tab order must follow document/reading order (report header → scope statement → per-metric sections → appendix) — don't reorder visually via CSS flex/grid `order` without matching DOM order, which breaks SC 2.4.3.\n- If tabs are used for metric categories, follow the WAI-ARIA APG tabs pattern (`role=\"tablist\"/\"tab\"/\"tabpanel\"`, arrow-key navigation) rather than plain JS show/hide `<div>`s.\n\n### 1.3 Alt text for base64-inlined SHAP charts (SC 1.1.1)\n**Gap:** base64-inlined `<img>` for SHAP force/summary plots is easy to ship with no `alt`, or a placeholder like `alt=\"chart\"` — both fail, and the second is arguably worse (false affordance of a description).\n**Fix:**\n- Every SHAP image needs a substantive `alt` generated from the same data driving the plot, not hand-written per report: e.g. `alt=\"SHAP summary plot for {metric_name}, subgroup {subgroup}: top contributing features are skin-tone proxy (+0.31), eye region texture (+0.18), lighting (-0.09)\"`.\n- Because this is a data visualization (not decorative), pair `alt` (short) with a `longdesc`-equivalent: an adjacent `<details><summary>Data table for this chart</summary>...</details>` or a `<table>` immediately following the image with the same feature/weight values — satisfies SC 1.1.1 without relying on `longdesc` (deprecated/unsupported by most AT).\n- Since output is audit-grade and zero-dependency, generate this alt text server-side from the SHAP `Explanation` object at report-build time (same pass that renders the PNG) — never leave it to manual authoring, or it silently drifts from the chart on every re-run.\n\n### 1.4 Semantic HTML / table structure (SC 1.3.1, SC 4.1.1/4.1.2)\n**Gap:** the Core Four metric table (metric_name, subgroup, n, point estimate, CI, p, insufficient_sample) is exactly the kind of dense data table that gets built as `<div>` grids with CSS for print-friendliness, which is invisible to screen readers.\n**Fix:**\n- Real `<table>` markup: `<caption>` per table (e.g. \"Disparate Impact Ratio by race subgroup — FairFace baseline\"), `<th scope=\"col\">` for the 8 schema columns, `<th scope=\"row\">` for the subgroup cell if subgroup is the row key.\n- For intersectional rows (`race=Black&gender=Female`), don't collapse the composite key into one opaque `<td>` string for sighted users only — expose it as two `scope=\"rowgroup\"` headers or a readable label (\"Black, Female\") with the raw key in a `data-*` attribute for machine reuse.\n- `insufficient_sample=True` rows: the `metric_value=None` cell must render as the word \"Not reported\" (or similar), never an empty `<td>` — empty cells are ambiguous to screen reader table navigation (missing data vs. zero vs. rendering bug).\n- Section headings must nest properly (`<h1>` report title → `<h2>` per metric family → `<h3>` per subgroup breakdown) with no skipped levels, so AT users can navigate by heading (SC 2.4.6/2.4.10).\n- `<html lang=\"en\">` at minimum; if i18n ships (Part 2), `lang` must be set per rendered locale, and any inline non-English regulatory citation gets `lang=\"...\"` on the span (SC 3.1.2).\n\n### 1.5 Print stylesheet (secondary, SC 1.4.10 adjacent)\n**Gap:** print media query is already planned — worth confirming it doesn't hide the alt-text/table-fallback content that only exists for AT (some print resets do `.sr-only{display:none}` for cleanliness, which is correct for print but must not leak into the screen stylesheet).\n**Fix:** keep `.sr-only` (visually-hidden-but-accessible) pattern for any AT-only text, confirm the print media query only affects `@media print`, not the base screen styles.\n\n### 1.6 Audit summary table\n\n| WCAG SC | Area | Status if unaddressed | Required fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.1.1 | SHAP chart alt text | Fail | Auto-generated substantive alt + adjacent data table |\n| 1.3.1 | Metric table semantics | Fail | Real `<table>`/`<th scope>`, no empty cells |\n| 1.4.1 | Color-only flags | Fail | Text/icon + color, never color alone |\n| 1.4.11 | Flag badge contrast | Fail (likely) | 3:1 non-text contrast, defined palette |\n| 2.1.1 | Interactive elements | Fail (if Track 26 ships click-only divs) | Native `<button>`/`<details>`, keyboard operable |\n| 2.4.6/2.4.10 | Heading structure | Fail (if flat) | Strict `<h1>→<h2>→<h3>` nesting |\n| 2.4.7 | Focus visibility | Fail (if `outline:none`) | Visible `:focus-visible` style |\n| 3.1.2 | Language of parts | Fail once i18n ships | `lang` attr per locale/citation |\n| 4.1.2 | Custom widget semantics | Fail (if custom tabs) | ARIA tabs pattern or native elements |\n\nNone of these require new external dependencies — all fixable within the existing zero-CDN, self-contained-HTML constraint.\n\n---\n\n## Part 2 — i18n Scaffold\n\n### 2.1 Constraint restated\nNo CDN-hosted i18n JS libraries (violates the zero-external-dependency, self-contained-HTML design). Translation must happen at **report-generation time** (Python/Jinja2), not client-side in the browser.\n\n### 2.2 String externalization strategy\nUse Python's stdlib `gettext` — no new runtime dependency, no CDN, works natively with Jinja2 via the `jinja2.ext.i18n` extension (ships with Jinja2, already a project dependency).\n\n```\nreport/\n  i18n/\n    messages.pot          # extracted source strings (pybabel/xgettext)\n    en/LC_MESSAGES/messages.po|.mo\n    es/LC_MESSAGES/messages.po|.mo\n    ...\n  generator.py\n  templates/\n    report.html.j2\n```\n\n- Enable in the Jinja2 `Environment`: `extensions=[\"jinja2.ext.i18n\"]`, then `env.install_gettext_translations(translation_obj)` per render call, selected by a `locale` param passed into `generate_report(..., locale=\"en\")`.\n- Template strings wrapped with `{% trans %}...{% endtrans %}` (or `_(\"...\")` for simple strings), extracted with `pybabel extract` into `messages.pot`, translated `.po` files compiled to `.mo` at build time — **build-time-only dependency** (`Babel` in `dev`/build extras, not shipped in the rendered HTML, so it doesn't violate the no-CDN constraint since nothing is fetched by the browser).\n- Numbers/dates: `Babel`'s `format_decimal`/`format_percent` for locale-correct grouping/decimal separators on point estimates and percentages — but see 2.3, this needs a carve-out for statistical notation.\n- Output artifact stays a single self-contained `.html` file per locale (`report_en.html`, `report_es.html`), generated from the same template + data — no runtime language switcher needed, no client-side JS i18n library, consistent with the current architecture.\n\n### 2.3 What's translatable vs. what must stay fixed\n\n**Translate (UI chrome / narrative prose):**\n- Section headers (\"Executive Summary\", \"Methodology\", \"Limitations\", \"Regulatory Mapping\")\n- Narrative sentences (scope-boundary statement, dataset description prose, SHAP explanation text)\n- Table captions and column header *labels* (the human-readable header text, not the underlying field name)\n- Alt text for charts (Part 1.3) — generate the templated sentence in the target locale, keep the numeric values as-is\n- Legends, footnotes, print-mode headers/footers\n\n**Must stay in English / fixed notation (regulatory + statistical precision):**\n- `metric_name` literal values (`demographic_parity_difference`, `equalized_odds_difference`, `equal_opportunity_difference`, `disparate_impact_ratio`) — these are the schema-locked API/report contract (schema-lock-m1.md); translating them breaks any downstream machine parsing or cross-report comparison. Display a translated *label* next to the fixed literal, don't replace it: `Demographic Parity Difference (demographic_parity_difference)`.\n- Statistical notation: `p`, `α = 0.05`, `95% CI`, `n ≥ 30` — internationally recognized notation; translating \"p-value\" prose is fine, but the symbols/thresholds themselves stay as-is.\n- EU AI Act article citations (`Art. 10(2)–10(5)`) and NIST RMF category names (`Govern`, `Map`, `Measure`, `Monitor`) — these are proper-noun references to specific legal/framework text; translating them risks misrepresenting the citation. Keep the citation fixed, translate the surrounding sentence.\n- Demographic label *values* (`White`, `Black`, `Latino_Hispanic`, `East Asian`, `Southeast Asian`, `Indian`, `Middle Eastern`, `Male`, `Female`, age bins) — these are FairFace's locked taxonomy (schema-lock-m1.md), used as literal keys in `subgroup` composite strings and in any downstream data export. A localized *display* label may be shown for readability (e.g. \"Blanco\" next to \"White\" in an `es` report), but the canonical English value must remain present (e.g. as a `data-race=\"White\"` attribute or in parentheses) so the audit trail and any re-ingestion of the report's data stay schema-conformant.\n- File/variable-facing content: `image_id` values, raw CSV/JSON field names in any appendix — never translated.\n\n### 2.4 Rationale for the split\nRegulatory audit reports have a dual audience: a compliance/legal reader (needs the exact statutory citation and metric name untouched, for cross-referencing against the regulation and against other audits) and a general stakeholder reader (needs the prose in their language). The scaffold above serves both without forking the schema — translation lives entirely in the *presentation* layer (Jinja2 template strings), never touches the *data* layer (`MetricResult`/`SubjectRecord` field names or enum values), so WP4's detection engine output and WP3's report template stay decoupled exactly as designed, and the locale is just another render-time parameter, not a schema variant.\n\n### 2.5 Effort/risk note\nBecause BiasAperture is diagnostic-only and the report is the only end-user-facing surface, i18n cost is bounded to template string extraction + one `.po` file per target language — no localization of any computational code, metric logic, or schema. Reasonable to scope as: (1) English baseline (done implicitly), (2) extraction pass + wrapping in Track 28 follow-up, (3) translation of `.po` files as a translator/non-engineering task, decoupled from the WP4/WP3 code timeline.\n",
